{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":187779,"reputation":17595,"user_id":2210,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":44,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/465a211b4981a6ae2b28df5c3c750bd9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"gablin","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/2210/gablin"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294909973,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1294909973,"creation_date":1294909973,"answer_id":36258,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/CogmS.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n\n<p><strong>Conceptual Blockbusting</strong> <em>by</em> James L. Adams</p>\n\n<p>A good book concerning problem solving.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":195138,"reputation":5659,"user_id":552,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":25,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f0b104c7bd21da536ed7aba8b35f344d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"LennyProgrammers","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/552/lennyprogrammers"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294909391,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1294909391,"creation_date":1294909391,"answer_id":36257,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0596101538\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Mind performance hacks</a></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/DSwRP.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":195138,"reputation":5659,"user_id":552,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":25,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f0b104c7bd21da536ed7aba8b35f344d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"LennyProgrammers","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/552/lennyprogrammers"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294909305,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1294909305,"creation_date":1294909305,"answer_id":36256,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0316178314\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Six thinking hats</a></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/qqj0L.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":53002,"reputation":9046,"user_id":12902,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":29,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/89c3645ed352c9f08fae80da29437236?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Pete","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/12902/pete"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294905018,"score":4,"last_activity_date":1294905018,"creation_date":1294905018,"answer_id":36240,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/Pragmatic-Thinking-Learning-Refactor-Programmers/dp/1934356050/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;s=books&amp;qid=1294904907&amp;sr=8-1\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Pragmatic Thinking And Learning</a> by Andrew Hunt. </p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/4n6ZQ.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n\n<p>Though the targeted audience is programmers, non-programmers can read it as well. It just uses some programming metaphors. But the book is really about how the brain functions, and how we can optimize how we use it.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":71093,"reputation":16146,"user_id":101,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/09941fead3a8de71294d0e5a4e0a84eb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Walter","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/101/walter"},"is_accepted":true,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":43,"last_activity_date":1294680175,"last_edit_date":1294680175,"creation_date":1283693537,"answer_id":985,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0465067107\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Design of Everyday Things</a> by Donald Norman</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/zHf58.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user12265"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294349322,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1294349322,"creation_date":1294349322,"answer_id":34404,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dan_Millman\" rel=\"nofollow\">Dan Millman's</a> \"Everyday Enlightenment\". A self-help book, whose title is far more esoteric than the advice it actually gives. It is clearly written and structured, encouraging, and actually helpful.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":65147,"reputation":11104,"user_id":12354,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7baab1a930992c97d5b8efd48071ace8?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Homde","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/12354/homde"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1294328547,"creation_date":1294328547,"answer_id":34283,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Stephen Kings - On Writing,  No I'm serious so stick with me! :) I realized that the coding process actually have a lot in coming with the authoring process. First you need to get things  down on paper/code, then you need to edit/refactor it. You need to let the book/code \"rest\" a little sometimes before coming back to it with new realizations etc etc</p>\n\n<p>Read the book and think about how it applies to coding</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":230362,"reputation":2444,"user_id":8360,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/ce109c88210d5c36c957082ddfe92d16?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"duros","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/8360/duros"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":0,"last_activity_date":1291648816,"creation_date":1291648816,"answer_id":24133,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://books.google.com/books?id=2qu8EK7ickIC&amp;printsec=frontcover&amp;dq=Russell+L.+Ackoff+the+art+of+problem+solving&amp;source=bl&amp;ots=Da0lC4iFCL&amp;sig=8qJvEcpAImgeSVpFIc-ZCIuDCis&amp;hl=en&amp;ei=3v78TLCiA9HpOeyrlNUK&amp;sa=X&amp;oi=book_result&amp;ct=result&amp;resnum=1&amp;ved=0CBoQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&amp;q&amp;f=false\" rel=\"nofollow\">The art of problem solving</a></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":26625,"reputation":11393,"user_id":4854,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/02e56b30e8b319fd2e29b13a778a3f7f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"TMN","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/4854/tmn"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1291647674,"creation_date":1291647674,"answer_id":24125,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><em>Alice In Wonderland</em> and <em>Through The Looking-Glass</em>.  Read Alice's conversation with the White Knight and see if you don't come away with a deeper appreciation for indirection and reference variables!</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":9520,"reputation":22009,"user_id":5512,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0bce4ecc3649443edeb6eba28f9a6c04?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Scott Whitlock","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/5512/scott-whitlock"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1288398285,"creation_date":1288398285,"answer_id":15705,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://www.cryptonomicon.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Cryptonomicon</a>:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/Qh2De.jpg\" alt=\"Cryptonomicon Cover Art\"></p>\n\n<p>Recommended for the morale boost.  :)</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":99054,"reputation":8243,"user_id":6329,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/z7FUt.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Macneil","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/6329/macneil"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1288066280,"creation_date":1288066280,"answer_id":14656,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>The single best book on writing I've ever read is <a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0205296327\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><em>The Sense of Structure: Writing from the Reader's Perspective</em></a>.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/w2esj.jpg\" alt=\"The Sense of Structure\"></p>\n\n<p>It can help you think logically about your writing. It gives concrete advise about the structure of what you say. It's not a book about grammar mistakes or rules, nor a bunch of platitudes-- which distinguishes itself from most other writing books. [At least that I've seen, and I've read \"the classics\" too.]</p>\n\n<p>Better writing can help you compose better emails, write write clearer comments, and help you realize what you think it important or not.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":43037,"reputation":2245,"user_id":4982,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5dcf9c84cdc5d8e39116b880187afbbd?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Huperniketes","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/4982/huperniketes"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1287034529,"last_edit_date":1287034529,"creation_date":1287006780,"answer_id":11833,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>While <a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0449905292\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Strategy of the Dolphin</a> is geared more towards adjusting one's mindset to solving problems arising from dealing with others, applying the adaptive skills in dealing with the absolute world of developer tools will help intuit new ways of addressing problems.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/uS8gr.jpg\" alt=\"Strategy of the Dolphin\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":43037,"reputation":2245,"user_id":4982,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5dcf9c84cdc5d8e39116b880187afbbd?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Huperniketes","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/4982/huperniketes"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1287032567,"creation_date":1287032567,"answer_id":11893,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1561585335\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Patterns of Home</a></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/sHusD.jpg\" alt=\"Patterns of Home front cover\"></p>\n\n<p>An excellent reference on 10 patterns for the <em>process</em> of designing homes. Instead of being a catalog of patterns for elements of a home (such as living room, alcove, patio, etc.), it's a catalog of principles that should shape the design of homes; a guide to the factors that are critical to making a home livable for its habitants.</p>\n\n<p>I've translated the 10 design process patterns they catalog to the design of software and it's remarkable how effective they are in helping you focus on the details relevant to big-picture thinking before you get bogged down on the specifics of designing and coding classes.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":58302,"reputation":647,"user_id":4954,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e06dc7c688442a2a8f269b432aac977c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Lasse Espeholt","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/4954/lasse-espeholt"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":27,"last_activity_date":1286701670,"last_edit_date":1495543226,"creation_date":1286701670,"answer_id":10817,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p><strong><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1439167346\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">How to win friends and influence people</a></strong></p>\n\n<p>As mentioned here: <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38210\">What non-programming books should programmers read?</a> \"How to win friends and influence people\" is a VERY good book.</p>\n\n<p><em>You don't acquire programming skills directly by this book, but indirectly I'll argue that you do.</em> If you're not very good at dealing with people this will help you with socializing with people that may influence you and your programming skills.</p>\n\n<p>The title of the book may sound a little superficial but it´s not. You learn what others do naturally, and Dale Carnegie encourage you not to be superficial.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Worth a read :-)</strong></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/NUA5r.jpg\" alt=\"How to win friends and influence people\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user1539"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1285477954,"creation_date":1285477954,"answer_id":7391,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1400064287\" rel=\"nofollow\">Made to Stick</a> - Absolutely essential for building skills on getting your ideas across to others (especially non-programmers) and looking at things from others' (read customers') perspectives.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":36,"last_activity_date":1285459500,"last_edit_date":1285459500,"creation_date":1283997648,"answer_id":1597,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Getting Things Done</p>\n\n<p>David Allen</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/fQUUy.jpg\" alt=\"Cover\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":52432,"reputation":39480,"user_id":897,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2e236942ba8d664b677cbde3ee69986c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"GrandmasterB","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/897/grandmasterb"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1285355672,"creation_date":1285355672,"answer_id":7164,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>This is a very inspirational book on creativity.  Something sorely needed in this 'new' tech world full of 'best practices' and 'accepted methodologies'.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/bHLJD.jpg\" alt=\"Ignore Everybody\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":13,"last_activity_date":1285352053,"last_edit_date":1285352053,"creation_date":1284242939,"answer_id":2919,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance</p>\n\n<p>Robert Pirsig</p>\n\n<p>It teach us how to do right maintenance on software.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/5q0oN.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":110758,"reputation":676,"user_id":2448,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9017e885805fa6489bdc714e23dbd9d6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe D","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/2448/joe-d"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1284888928,"creation_date":1284888928,"answer_id":5261,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>I'm surprised nobody has suggested any fiction. I would have though reading some fiction every now and then would improve your thinking skills. Anyway, here's my 2c.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy\n  is a wholly remarkable book. Perhaps\n  the most remarkable, certainly the\n  most successful book ever to come out\n  of the great publishing corporations\n  of Ursa Minor - of which no Earthman\n  had ever heard of. More popular than\n  the Celestial Home Care Omnibus,\n  better selling than Fifty-Three More\n  Things to do in Zero Gravity, and more\n  controversial than Oolon Colluphid's\n  trilogy of philosophical blockbusters\n  Where God Went Wrong, Some More of\n  God's Greatest Mistakes and Who is\n  this God Person Anyway? It's already\n  supplanted the Encyclopedia Galactica\n  as the standard repository of all\n  knowledge and wisdom, for two\n  important reasons. First, it's\n  slightly cheaper; and secondly it has\n  the words <strong>DON'T PANIC</strong> printed in\n  large friendly letters on its cover.</p>\n</blockquote>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":94344,"reputation":3259,"user_id":1373,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/El2tc.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Chankey Pathak","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/1373/chankey-pathak"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":-3,"last_activity_date":1284273186,"last_edit_date":1592301709,"creation_date":1284273186,"answer_id":2997,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<ul>\n<li><p>Code complete</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>The Pragmatic Programmer</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":2,"last_activity_date":1284243412,"creation_date":1284243412,"answer_id":2924,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Math Refresher for Scientists and Engineers</p>\n\n<p>John R. Fanchi </p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/ht5Mq.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":19,"last_activity_date":1284242658,"last_edit_date":1284242658,"creation_date":1283997344,"answer_id":1596,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>Peopleware: Productive Projects and Teams\nTom DeMarco and Timothy Lister</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/oYrWn.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":16,"last_activity_date":1284242583,"last_edit_date":1284242583,"creation_date":1283997079,"answer_id":1595,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People\nStephen Covey</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/kl3Ux.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":77792,"reputation":10816,"user_id":389,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/23y8L.png?s=256","display_name":"AArteDoCodigo.com.br - Maniero","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/389/aartedocodigo-com-br-maniero"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":21,"last_activity_date":1284242469,"last_edit_date":1284242469,"creation_date":1283996782,"answer_id":1593,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>The Art of War\nSun Tzu</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/uPQuW.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":70017,"reputation":17505,"user_id":285,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/1b9f56786ceba3c57d0d2fe56182a4d3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Kate Gregory","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/285/kate-gregory"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1284217334,"creation_date":1284217334,"answer_id":2849,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/5bbMv.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n\n<p>A Pattern Language: Towns, Buildings, Construction</p>\n\n<p>It will teach you how to see that wildly disparate things can have underlying patterns in common that can be applied in an entirely different context. The aspect of a CEO's office that makes it a great office can be present in an undergrad's residence room, or missing from your million dollar mansion. Once you get this concept, you can apply it to software, as the Gang of Four did, and you will understand Design Patterns so much more completely. You will also have nicer living and working spaces for the rest of your life.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":109614,"reputation":5805,"user_id":1256,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9798931483c6fe5af55ec68698ffd0a0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Evan Plaice","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/1256/evan-plaice"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":8,"last_activity_date":1284209331,"creation_date":1284209331,"answer_id":2830,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Predictably_Irrational\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Predictably Irrational</a></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/63Nfd.jpg\" alt=\"Predictably Irrational\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":55521,"reputation":111,"user_id":661,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b1149eff135cf53ea5ac3b92eabb77b6?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Nilloc","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/661/nilloc"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1284041796,"creation_date":1284041796,"answer_id":1845,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0672316498\" rel=\"nofollow\">The Inmates are Running the Asylum</a> has been helpful to me during the planning stage of any software, or interaction project.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":26013,"reputation":841,"user_id":542,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/95e28ac250c4604a6f9d7e34705c3259?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Richard","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/542/richard"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":7,"last_activity_date":1284025591,"creation_date":1284025591,"answer_id":1724,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>I'm going to recommend a triplet of books that are about being a programmer (rather than programming), or a leader of programmers. While all are over 30 years since their original publication all are (especially with updates, commentary in their anniversary editions):</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>\"<a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0201835959\">The Mythical Man Month</a>\", Brooks.</li>\n<li>\"<a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0932633439\">Peopleware</a>\", BeMarco &amp; Lister.</li>\n<li>\"<a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0932633420\">The Psychology of Computer Programming</a>\", Weinberg (the PL/1 code is easy to follow, and can easily be applied to edge cases of any programming language).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":21051,"reputation":676,"user_id":305,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e74a20bb1502e262f6ac53ea91dfd2ff?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"helgeg","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/305/helgeg"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":3,"last_activity_date":1283985884,"creation_date":1283985884,"answer_id":1471,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>\"How to solve it\" by George Pólya. While targeted for mathematics, the problem solving strategies can readily be applied to programming.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":67735,"reputation":4114,"user_id":64,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0bf5f81f9852861944d7306696884226?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Gordon","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/64/gordon"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":25,"last_activity_date":1283763954,"creation_date":1283763954,"answer_id":1101,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0789723107\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Don't Make Me Think! A Common Sense Approach to Web Usability</a></p>\n\n<p>Relevant to all user interface design.</p>\n\n<p>Also includes general common sense for design that every programmer should know.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/yq0II.jpg\" alt=\"Don&#39;t Make me think\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":40545,"reputation":11120,"user_id":38,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2bacff9b8c4ef850c9980b66c7cc2689?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paddyslacker","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/38/paddyslacker"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":31,"last_activity_date":1283757719,"creation_date":1283757719,"answer_id":1099,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/0394756827\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid</a> by <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_Hofstadter\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Douglas Hofstadter</a>. </p>\n\n<p>If you haven't read this, put it on your list. It's still breathtaking over 30 years later. While <a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/B001FA23HM\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">I am a Strange Loop</a> contains a more complete and convincing argument about where consciousness comes from, GEB explores the realms of symmetry, intelligence and logic systems through the music of Bach, the mathematics of Gödel and the art of Escher. It's as infuriating and entertaining as it sounds.</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/BEXY3.jpg\" alt=\"Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":158513,"reputation":4400,"user_id":149,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f80c58d49a2d79fb5f65957bf13712f2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"George Marian","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/149/george-marian"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":5,"last_activity_date":1283711016,"creation_date":1283711016,"answer_id":1016,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/B0020MMBCG\" rel=\"noreferrer\">The Back of the Napkin</a>, by <a href=\"http://www.thebackofthenapkin.com/\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Dan Roam</a>.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://www.coverbrowser.com/image/bestsellers-2008/302-7.jpg\" rel=\"noreferrer\">The Back of the Napkin http://www.coverbrowser.com/image/bestsellers-2008/302-7.jpg</a></p>\n\n<p>A great book about visual thinking techniques.  There is also an <a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1591843065\" rel=\"noreferrer\">expanded</a> edition now.  I can't speak to that version, as I do not own it; yet.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":86469,"reputation":25869,"user_id":6,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/14a4ec3cdc51bfd7e6f4d8a61e007592?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Fishtoaster","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/6/fishtoaster"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1283702634,"creation_date":1283702634,"answer_id":1001,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p><a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Soul_of_a_New_Machine\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Soul of a New Machine</a></p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/SOoqp.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":2279,"reputation":7027,"user_id":24,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/84af4c25398f072ce1462795fd67bf49?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Brian R. Bondy","link":"https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/users/24/brian-r-bondy"},"is_accepted":false,"community_owned_date":1294328547,"score":6,"last_activity_date":1283688895,"creation_date":1283688895,"answer_id":982,"question_id":966,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 2.5","body":"<p>I would say any introductory book on graphic design would be useful for a programmer and help them while programming for some aspects of their work.</p>\n\n<p>For example: </p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1566091594\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Non-Designer's Design Book: Design and Typographic Principles for the Visual Novice</a></p>\n\n<p><a href=\"http://rads.stackoverflow.com/amzn/click/1566091594\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/tqKkx.jpg\" alt=\"alt text\"></a></p>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":288}